From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 06:26:49 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Embedding: deliver unmasked SIMD floating-point exceptions

x86 faults with #XM when an SSE or AVX instruction raises an exception
MXCSR unmasks, before writing its destination, and an embedding host
that runs x86 programs exactly needs to know when. The floating-point
mode an embedder sets now also says whether MXCSR unmasks only some of
invalid operation, division by zero and overflow, or any other
exception, and LDMXCSR, FXRSTOR and XRSTOR leave a context whose mode
they change.

While MXCSR unmasks an exception, every SIMD floating-point instruction
has an EmbedderFPCheck op, and comparisons, COMIS, conversions,
roundings and half-precision conversions get one that only settles
exceptions. With only the first three unmasked, a check tests in line
what could raise them: a result whose exponent is all ones or one
below, a NaN source of a comparison, MIN, MAX, rounding or widening, a
source a conversion cannot hold, and a DPPS or DPPD source large enough
to overflow. Otherwise every check goes to the host's helper, which
settles the instruction's exceptions and may end the dispatch with
EMBEDDER_EXIT_FP_FAULT. An instruction checked in several parts marks
them, and the host settles its exceptions at the last; when a part that
no test sent to the helper may hold flags of masked exceptions,
EMBEDDER_EXIT_FP_RETRY asks the host to run the instruction again as a
unit of its own, which ExecuteEmbedded() now translates in a
floating-point mode it names.
